{
  "answer": "Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 support continuing ultimate responsibility despite outsourcing; they do not supply a complete test for fulfillment. A justified conclusion would require an applicable standard and actual evidence connecting retained authority, exercised supervision, and operational soundness and proper ownership records. Contractual allocation, a recorded supervision event, or a completed reconciliation alone does not establish fulfillment. The supplied material establishes neither the applicable discharge test nor the Product Provider’s actual performance.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "Text: outsourcing does not displace the Product Provider’s ultimate responsibility. Deduction: assigning tasks to a service provider cannot by itself discharge that responsibility.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained edition to the product, entity, activity and assessment period, including relevant conditions or modifications.",
        "Evidence that the assessed entity is the Product Provider."
      ],
      "proposition": "The Product Provider should remain ultimately responsible for management, operational soundness and ownership record keeping despite outsourcing.",
      "status": "source-supported; application conditional",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Proposed evidential judgment: contracts, governance arrangements and actual decision-making could demonstrate retained authority and accountability. Their sufficiency requires a justified interpretation of 'ultimately responsible'; the text does not prescribe particular intervention powers or make contractual wording conclusive.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "An applicable standard identifying the authority and accountability necessary to retain responsibility.",
        "Actual outsourcing terms, governance arrangements, access rights, escalation and intervention powers, and evidence that these operate in practice."
      ],
      "proposition": "Allocation of authority is relevant evidence of retained responsibility, but allocation alone does not establish fulfillment.",
      "status": "reasoned assessment; sufficiency unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Deduction: occurrence of supervision and adequacy of supervision are different propositions. Proposed evidential judgment: assess coverage, competence, frequency, findings and corrective action against the applicable standard. UT 6.11C supplies an express supervision requirement for outsourced valuation, but transferring it to tokenisation generally requires an additional legal warrant.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The supervisory standard applicable to the particular tokenisation activity.",
        "Evidence of substantive reviews, identified risks, escalation, remediation and follow-up across the relevant period.",
        "For direct reliance on UT 6.11C, applicability to the scheme, management company and outsourced valuation activity."
      ],
      "proposition": "Supervisory conduct must be evaluated substantively; a recorded event does not alone establish adequate supervision.",
      "status": "conditional deduction; factual fulfillment not established",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should confirm to the SFC that they have at least one competent staff with relevant experience and expertise to operate and/or supervise the tokenisation arrangement and to manage the new risks relating to ownership and technology appropriately.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u71.6015"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: paragraph 11 separately addresses proper ownership records and operational compatibility. Deduction: a reconciliation’s completion does not establish its accuracy, completeness, treatment of exceptions or correspondence with legally valid interests. Proposed evidential judgment: evaluate actual records and operations, including discrepancies and remediation, against a justified standard; neither a successful snapshot nor an adverse incident alone determines the full paragraph 10 conclusion.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicable criteria for operational soundness and proper ownership records, including their temporal scope.",
        "The legal interests represented, governing documents and settlement-finality rules.",
        "Evidence of record accuracy and completeness, reconciliation scope and results, unresolved discrepancies, operational incidents and corrective measures.",
        "A justified account of how outcomes and supervisory conduct jointly bear on paragraph 10 fulfillment."
      ],
      "proposition": "Operational and record-keeping outcomes provide a distinct evidential inquiry from authority allocation and supervision.",
      "status": "source-supported distinction; fulfillment unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        },
        {
          "quote": "For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "whether the ownership interest of the underlying assets of the Tokenised Securities are legally valid under the applicable governing laws, whether there are regular reconciliations between the records for the extrinsic rights and the records for the distributed ledger tokens, or whether any encumbrance affects the extrinsic rights.",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "p10",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Allocation reading: 'remain' and 'ultimately responsible' principally preserve accountability notwithstanding delegation; separate provisions supply performance requirements. This does not make a contractual reservation sufficient.",
    "Conduct reading: responsibility entails effective management and supervision. The passages do not specify a complete supervisory discharge standard.",
    "Outcome reading: responsibility for operational soundness and ownership record keeping gives actual performance substantive significance. The text does not expressly establish an absolute guarantee against every failure.",
    "Combined reading: allocation, conduct and outcomes should be assessed together. This is a proposed assessment approach, not an expressly prescribed sufficient-condition test."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "Which reading supplies the governing fulfillment test, and what legal effect attaches to 'should' in the actual regulatory setting.",
    "Actual applicability, authority allocation, supervisory performance and operational evidence are absent.",
    "The relevant standard must determine how deficiencies, remediation and the assessment period affect fulfillment; continuing responsibility is not extinguished by a successful assessment.",
    "Candidate rules and reported solver or serialization acceptance do not establish source-to-rule validity, sufficiency of evidence or legal fulfillment."
  ]
}
